PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.630
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.550
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.660
I'm Priyanka, and
in this episode,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.720
we're wrapping up the mobile
backend app recipe with Jen.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.624
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.287 --> 00:00:21.870
JEN PERSON: It's
bittersweet that this

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.700
is our last mobile
app backend recipe.

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.625
I've had so much fun
cooking with you.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.000
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Ah, well, it's

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.640
been great having
you visit my kitchen.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.009
So tell me about
this last recipe.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.630
What are we cooking up today?

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.380
JEN PERSON: Today's solution
combines REST or gRPC

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:38.572
with Compute Engine.

00:00:38.572 --> 00:00:40.530
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Ah,
so the ingredients then,

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:45.170
are some sort of API with REST
or gRPC and Compute Engine?

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:46.310
JEN PERSON: Exactly.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:48.435
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So I
talked about Compute Engine

00:00:48.435 --> 00:00:49.290
in the first season.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.050
I'll link the video below
for any of our viewers

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:53.340
who didn't catch it.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.730
Well, Compute Engine lets you
create and run virtual machines

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.590
on Google infrastructure.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.500
You have admin rights to
the server and full control

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.019
over its configuration,
but this also

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.530
means that you are responsible
for updates and maintenance.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.850
JEN PERSON: Exactly, Priyanka.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.880
Compute Engine gives you
more configuration control

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.000
than my previous recipes,
but that puts the onus

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.800
on the developer to
monitor and maintain it.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.650
Now there are two
main protocols used

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.120
to connect to Compute Engine
instances, REST and gRPC.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.730
Like all of my recipes, the
choice you make here really

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.437
depends on your app's
particular needs.

00:01:29.437 --> 00:01:31.020
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So I know that Rest

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.880
is an architecture
for network app

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.170
that uses an HTTP request to
post, read and delete data.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.830
You can build REST APIs on
Compute Engine, the App Engine

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.070
standard environment, and the
App Engine flexible environment

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.830
instances that your
app can call to access

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.480
the backend service you build.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.443
In this case, we're
specifying Compute Engine.

00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:52.110
JEN PERSON: Yes.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.850
And gRPC is a
framework that makes

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.250
it possible for a mobile
app to directly call

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.090
methods on a backend service
as if it were a local object.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:03.210
gRPC uses the HTTP/2
standard, which

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:06.480
introduces bi-directional
streaming, flow control, header

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.139
compression, and the ability
to multiplex requests

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.280
over a single TCP connection.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.620
You can use gRPC to make
your mobile app more

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.930
bandwidth efficient
and to reduce latency

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.170
between your app and backend
service running on GCP.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.630
You can write gRPC
clients and servers

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.370
in any of gRPC's
supported languages.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.200
So you could, for instance,
create a gRPC server in Java

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.067
with clients in
Go, Python or Ruby.

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:33.150
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: That
is great overview of gRPC.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.420
We also talked about REST
and Compute Engine so far.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.510
In which scenario would
you recommend this recipe?

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.630
JEN PERSON: I recommend Compute
Engine with REST or gRPC

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.210
for porting an existing
backend service running

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:48.180
on an on premises server
or a virtual machine,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.820
and backend services that
require a custom server

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.212
or third party libraries.

00:02:52.212 --> 00:02:53.670
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So this could be

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.430
good solution for
handling my legacy on prem

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.080
code for Priyanka's Kitchen.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.340
JEN PERSON: Exactly.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.710
But if your app requires
automatic real time

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:04.110
data synchronization
across devices,

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.135
I recommend using one of our
Firebase solutions instead.

00:03:07.135 --> 00:03:09.510
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: What if I
prefer automatic maintenance

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.410
and scaling?

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.990
JEN PERSON: Well, then this
isn't the solution for you,

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.630
since you must maintain and
upgrade the server yourself,

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.205
and you must manually configure
and manage in autoscaler.

00:03:18.205 --> 00:03:19.830
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Where can developers

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:23.290
learn more about using Compute
Engine with REST or gRPC

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:24.150
in their apps?

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.100
JEN PERSON: There is
an end-to-end sample

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.200
app called Sticky
Notes that sends text

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.830
to the service, which responds
by returning a generated image.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.500
The Sticky Notes sample
is available in both REST

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.842
and gRPC versions, so you can
compare the two protocols.

00:03:37.842 --> 00:03:40.050
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: All right,
Jen, that sounds great.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:43.690
I cannot wait to take a look
at the Sticky Notes sample app.

